From 0e8c4cb044c9a2b9917a0d5e50fe1d32d4a526e6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yekai pan <pyk@seas.upenn.edu>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 16:35:45 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 12/25] l2r-local: entry 23-d: worklist drops behind an option
 (worklist-drops)

Third step of the in-place worklist: the acquire/drop expansion can
release a RecordDropKind::Worklist record through the thread's drop
stack (23-a, 23-b) instead of a retcon coroutine. New pass option
`worklist-drops` (default off; it wins over `retcon-drops`). No pipeline
sets it yet.

With the option, per Worklist record type and atomic kind
(WorklistDrop.cpp):
- every drop of the record calls `<dtor>.worklist(ref)`, the entry:
  `reussir.drop.run @<dtor>.worklist.walk(ref)`;
- `<dtor>.worklist.walk(ref)` (marked `reussir.worklist_drop`) is the
  record's ordinary member walk, with one change: a member box of a
  Worklist record is decremented, and once its count reaches zero it is
  deferred with `reussir.rc.defer @<dtor>.worklist.release` instead of
  released by recursion (an immediate is skipped; an atomic box uses
  fetch_sub);
- `<dtor>.worklist.release(box)`, what the drain calls: the walk of the
  box's content, then the box's free (emitBoxFree, 23-c).

So the order of a drop is: a cell's members other than Worklist boxes at
once, in field order; the cell freed; its dead Worklist children later,
the last one first, as Lean's lean_del pops its to-do list. A drop
started inside the drain (by a foreign hook, say) returns before its dead
children are released; they are popped after the hook returns (native
Lean's nested lean_del completes instead). Native Lean also defers every
dead member, not only those of the cycle. Upstream's
retcon cleanup instead finishes each member's subtree before the next
member (source order); that order needs state per level of the value,
since a parent resumes after its first child's whole subtree, and this
one needs none for any one-sided chain.

A Worklist value reached through other glue calls its entry, which then
only walks (the thread already drains). That is bounded as long as glue
defers every box edge of the record's cycle that the type graph shows (a
recursion through a closure's captures is not seen, as before). This patch covers boxes in
record members, arrays and unlocked cells, which the walk expands inline
(TailDrop's helpers take only boxes of TailLoop records, which lie outside
a Worklist cycle). Two cases are left to 23-e: a box behind a nullable
member still goes through the entry and recurses per level, and a locked
cell's payload is released inline in its critical section (or, with
retcon-drops also on, in a separate locked-drop function, through the
entry).

The retcon destructors are untouched; they are only bypassed for
Worklist records when the option is on. The glue functions take their
argument `noalias`, as the outlined destructors do.

Test: conversion/worklist_drop.mlir (entry, walk with deferrals and an
inline member release, release; with and without retcon-drops).
